1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,717
Ten parts, three operators, three trials each. Sixty numbers,

2
00:00:04,817 --> 00:00:08,588
and two completely different ways to reduce them.

3
00:00:08,683 --> 00:00:12,748
One reduces them with ranges and a printed table. The

4
00:00:12,848 --> 00:00:16,599
other with sums of squares and nothing looked up.

5
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:21,305
On a well-behaved study they land in the same place. Which is

6
00:00:21,405 --> 00:00:26,337
exactly why the older method survived in the plants for decades -

7
00:00:26,437 --> 00:00:29,975
it is not wrong, and it is much easier by hand.

8
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:36,702
Here are the three operators again, with each part's own average

9
00:00:36,802 --> 00:00:40,714
taken out, so what is left is the operator pattern.

10
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:44,526
With no interaction the lines are parallel. Each

11
00:00:44,626 --> 00:00:47,396
operator has an offset and keeps it.

12
00:00:47,483 --> 00:00:53,014
Now turn the interaction up. The offsets stop being constant: an operator who

13
00:00:53,114 --> 00:00:57,768
reads low on one part reads high on another, and the lines cross.

14
00:00:57,868 --> 00:01:02,303
That crossing is the whole of it. There is nothing else in the

15
00:01:02,403 --> 00:01:05,887
study that shows it, and the F test goes with it.

16
00:01:07,783 --> 00:01:11,843
The other thing ANOVA gives you is an identity.

17
00:01:11,943 --> 00:01:15,915
Take the total variation in all sixty numbers.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,754
It splits into exactly four pieces: the parts differing

19
00:01:19,854 --> 00:01:23,749
from each other, the operators differing from each other,

20
00:01:23,849 --> 00:01:27,324
the interaction between them, and the repeat error.

21
00:01:27,416 --> 00:01:31,028
And nothing is left over. Not approximately - the

22
00:01:31,128 --> 00:01:35,043
remainder is zero to floating point. That identity is

23
00:01:35,143 --> 00:01:39,361
what makes this a decomposition rather than a convention,

24
00:01:39,461 --> 00:01:43,300
and average-and-range has no such statement to make.

25
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:49,425
So if the older method has no term for it, where does the

26
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:54,392
interaction end up? The answer is that it does not end up anywhere.

27
00:01:55,383 --> 00:02:00,035
The white line is the truth, and it climbs as the interaction

28
00:02:00,135 --> 00:02:05,488
grows. ANOVA follows it. The older method does not move, because there

29
00:02:05,588 --> 00:02:10,163
is no term in its arithmetic for the thing that is changing.

30
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:15,012
Which means the gauge comes out looking better than it is. Not

31
00:02:15,112 --> 00:02:20,110
noisier. Better. That is the failure mode nobody warns you about.

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,607
One study cannot settle this - on the study we

33
00:02:25,707 --> 00:02:30,765
just watched, ANOVA actually overshot. So here is the comparison

34
00:02:30,865 --> 00:02:36,084
done properly: three hundred studies at each interaction strength.

35
00:02:36,183 --> 00:02:41,171
ANOVA stays within a few percent of the truth the whole way.

36
00:02:41,266 --> 00:02:45,335
Average-and-range walks away from it, and by the time

37
00:02:45,435 --> 00:02:49,818
the interaction matches the operator term it is reporting

38
00:02:49,918 --> 00:02:52,806
a gauge forty three percent too small.

39
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,856
So the decomposition has four terms and one of them is

40
00:02:56,956 --> 00:03:02,040
invisible to the older arithmetic. Which leaves the question this has

41
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:06,998
been building to. We have variances. A verdict needs a percentage,

42
00:03:07,098 --> 00:03:11,280
and a percentage needs a denominator. That is level four.

